The skill should deeply audit a live website, local codebase, or both, then create a raw Markdown file called website-audit.md.

This should be an intentionally messy, extremely detailed first-pass audit. Do not worry about making it polished, concise, client-ready, or perfectly organized. Capture as much useful information as possible so we can refine and restructure it later.

Audit anything relevant, including:
• UX
• UI design
• Layout
• Visual hierarchy
• Navigation
• Information architecture
• User journeys
• Messaging
• Content
• CTAs
• Conversion
• Trust
• Accessibility
• Mobile responsiveness
• Performance
• Consistency
• Interaction design
• Error states
• Empty states
• Forms
• Search
• Filters
• Onboarding
• Information density
• Cognitive load
• User friction
• Edge cases
• Missing pages or states
• Confusing decisions
• Potential opportunities

For every finding, include as much as possible:
• What you noticed
• Where it appears
• Why it may be a problem
• User impact
• Business impact
• Severity
• Possible solution
• Implementation notes
• Anything else worth investigating

Use headings, bullets, tables, notes, questions, rough ideas and unfinished thoughts freely.

Include duplicate or overlapping findings when useful. Do not remove details just to make the report cleaner.

Do not invent facts or analytics. Clearly label assumptions and anything that could not be verified.

Do not change the website. Only generate the detailed Markdown audit.
